Take the relation length off the extension lock.

Extending a relation under zou wrote the fork's new length to the
store from the backend, four bytes, synchronously, inside the relation
extension lock every other backend wanting to extend the same relation
was queued on. On server3 with a MinIO store that is 2359 puts of four
bytes in a 45 second insert run, against 4358 puts for the pages
themselves, each a network round trip, and Lock/extend is 43 per cent
of the writing backends' time, the largest single wait.

The length a running cluster reads already comes from the shared table
0004 added, so the store write is only there for what a restart reads.
An extension now leaves the new length in that table and
CheckPointGuts writes every length the store has not heard to the
store, inside the checkpoint that is about to move the redo floor past
the records replay would otherwise rebuild it from.

That leaves one rule to keep by hand that a file system gives away for
free, where a write past the end is what makes the file longer: a page
object must never reach the store ahead of a length that names it, or
a restore reads the short length and the pages past it are lost. So
every path that puts a page covers it first, writing the fork's whole
current length if the store is behind, which a relation being appended
to pays once and then not again until the writes catch up with the
end. smgrextend carries a page itself and stays eager for the same
reason.

A create settles a length before it drops the entry. Recovery calls
smgrcreate for every buffer it reads, and an entry dropped with a
length only shared memory held leaves no checkpoint to write it and no
cover to see it, while the pages it names are already in the store.
Settling first puts the length back on the extension's own rate for
the replay path, which is where it was before.

Truncate and unlink still write and delete the SIZE object eagerly and
now take a flush lock while they do, so a checkpoint's put cannot land
on top of a length just lowered or a fork just dropped. Outside a
postmaster, and when the shared table is full, extensions keep writing
the length on the spot.

